From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: PHIL: Nietzsche

> Tim May, you have mentioned your admiration for the German philosopher
> Friedrich Nietzsche.  I, and quite possibly others as well, am
> interested in reading some of his works.  I am familiar with some of
> Nietzsche's ideas, passages, quotes.  But which book(s) should one
> start out with, in what order should one read them in?  And if you
> could give brief justifications for such, I would appreciate it.
..
> -Han Y. Huang                                           hhuang@mit.edu

No, I don't plan to write up a Nietzsche reading list. Sorry.

Folks will be interested in different aspects of his ideas, so there
is no syllabus to follow. Best to just flip through the many paperback
editions of his writings until something looks interesting.

And it wouldn't hurt to read the Encyclopedia Britannica entries on
Nietzsche first to get an overview of his views and how they fit in
with those of Kierkegaard, Hegel, Husserl, Kant, Heidegger, etc.

"Also Sprach Zarathustra" ("Thus Spake/Spoke Zarathustra") is very
poetic, about a Seeker of Truth. "Beyond Good and Evil" is more about
morality. And so forth.

Good luck to all.

--Tim May


