Subject: BRIEFLY... Dec 1993

LIBERTARIAN WINS NOBEL PRIZE

The winner of the 1993 Nobel prize in chemistry has announced publicly 
that he's a libertarian. Kary Mullis, 48, won the prize for figuring 
out how to make unlimited copies of DNA. In an interview with the Fort 
Bragg Advocate News in California, a reporter noted that Mullis "talks 
about his Libertarian philosophy" as just one of "his wildly divergent 
interests. Government, he feels, is best suited to protect groups of 
people from outside interference, but ill-suited to dictate how 
individuals should lead their private lives." Another self-proclaimed 
libertarian Nobel Prize winner is Milton Friedman, recipient of the 
1973 honor for economics.

CATO ON CABLE

The Cato Institute, the largest libertarian thinktank in the country, 
cablecast the first installment of its new weekly television program 
"The Cato Forum" on December 9 on the National Empowerment Television 
(NET) Network. For those of you with satellite dishes, it airs Thursday 
nights at 7pm. For those of you who don't, please encourage your local 
cable network to pick up NET.

MILKEN SHRUGGED

"Free and full of ideas, Michael Milken would have us believe his 
prison time was only another pragmatic deal in a life of dealing. He 
can be amused in recalling parts of it. Among the jailhouse sustenance 
offered by outside fans were 22 copies of "Atlas Shrugged," the cult 
novel by Ayn Rand about willful, Promethean characters. 'I had not read 
it since the 60's,' says Milken, chatting about teaching his fledgling 
successors all about Wall Street. 'In retrospect, I'd have been better 
off had I re-read it in the early 80's,' he adds, smiling and admitting 
he could have used a caution against hubris in that especially rich 
time right before his downfall."   ----- New York Times 10/16/93

HOUSTON ZONKS ZONING

"A proposal that would have brought zoning to the biggest city in the 
nation without it went down in defeat. Voters rejected zoning 52 
percent to 48 percent, marking the third time in 45 years that the 
public has quashed efforts to end hodgepodge development in Houston. 
Tuesday's vote had been considered zoning proponents' best chance yet. 
Opponents argued that zoning would restrict Houston's growth, increase 
the cost of living and enable mostly white neighborhoods to keep 
minorities out." --- Associated Press 11/3/93

FRANK ZAPPA, R.I.P.

Eclectic musician and composer Frank Zappa died of cancer on December 
11. Famous for his musical genius and satirical lyricism, Zappa was an 
astute political observer. What is less well-known is that he briefly 
considered a run for the Libertarian Party presidential ticket in 1987. 
He was approached by Robert Murphy of the LP's National Committee. 
Murphy visited Zappa at his home where Zappa expressed interest in the 
idea.  Zappa said that if he did run for president, he wouldn't go "on 
tour," but that he would use satellite technology and television to 
campaign. Zappa later decided against the idea but continued his 
political activism in other ways.

LICENSORSHIP

Alexandria, VA --- All Mark Steckbeck wanted to do was "create 
discourse" about government.  Now that the state has voided his vanity 
plate, he's making a federal case out of his brief treatise: "GOVT 
SUX."  Mark Steckbeck, 34, of Arlington, filed the U.S. District Court 
lawsuit Tuesday, saying the state
Department of Motor Vehicles violated his right to free speech. "What's 
at issue is to have the government mind its own business and stop 
directing people what to say just because people don't agree with it," 
said Steckbeck's attorney, Victor M. Glasberg.


--- END ---


