Date: Mon, 20 Dec 93 13:52:00 PST
From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Subject: Vinge's Technological Singularity

I finally read Vinge's "Technological Singularity" article in the
Whole Earth Review, Winter 1993.  I greatly enjoy Vinge's fiction, so
I may have expected too much from this article, but I was
dissapointed; the analysis was not particularly deep relative to
discussions on this list, for example.  But perhaps it can serve as a
focal point for us to discuss related issues.

First some quotes:

------

 Based on this [progress in computer hardware] trend, I believe that
 the creation of greater-than-human intelligence will occur during the
 next thirty years. ... When greater-than-human intelligence drives
 progress, that progress will be much more rapid ... [and would]
 involve the creation of still more intelligent entitites - on a still
 shorter time scale. ...  Now, by creating the means to execute those
 [what-if] simulations at much higher speeds, ... This change will be a
 throwing-away of all the human rules, ... an exponential runaway ... a
 singularity ... where our old models must be discarded and a new
 reality rules. ...

 Stan Ulam paraphrased John von Neumann as saying: `...
 ever-accelerating progress of technology and changes in the mode of
 human life ... which gives the appearance of approaching some
 essential singularity ... beyond which human affairs, as we know them,
 could not continue.' ... it appears he is thinking of normal progress
 ... For me the superhumanity is the essence of the Singularity.
 Without that we would get a glut of technical riches, never properly
 absorbed. ... [without superhumans,] we would find our hardwaree
 performance curvess beginning to level off - because of our inability
 to automate the design work ... it would also be an end of progress.

 [Running you ran a million times faster, you] would probably burn out
 in a few weeks of outside time.  `Strong superhumanity' would be more
 than cranking up the clock speed on a human-equivalent mind.  ... our
 best guesses about the post-Singularity world can be obtained by
 thinking on the nature of strong superhumanity.  ... every time our
 ability to access information and to communicate to others is
 improved, in some sense we have achieved an increase over natural
 intelligence.  ... it's very likely that IA [Intelligence
 Amplification] is a much easier road to ... superhumanity than pure AI.

 We will see automation replacing higher- and higher-level jobs ...
 The work that is truely productive is the domain of a steadily
 smaller and more elite fraction of humanity.  ... we will see the
 predictions of true technological unemployment finally coming true.

------

Now I think von Neumann was right on - accelerating progress suggests
a special point in time.  But I'm much less sure of most of the ways
Vinge goes beyond von Neumann.

Vinge seems to think that our individual intelligence is a limit on
accelerating progress - unless we each get smarter, progress will end.
He also seems to think that designing intelligence is relatively easy
- while we might fail at designing hardware, we should get superhuman
software soon after we have the hardware to support it.  And
superhumans should find it even easier to design super-duper-humans,
otherwise progress in improving intelligence might slow down rather
than speed up in a spectacular explosion.

But I see designing intelligence as harder than designing hardware,
though just making folks run faster seems less hard to me.  And I
don't see rising individual intelligence as the root of progress - why
can't progress continue as it has before by having more, richer, and
more knowledgeable people within better social institutions?  And if
superhumans come by amplifying ordinary intelligence, I don't see why
most folks couldn't remain productive.

Robin Hanson

