Blanc Weber asks about PPLs:

> So just what exactly is a PPL?

PPL stands for "privately-produced law" and is shorthand, possibly
coined only on this list (I first heard Tom Morrow using it), for a
constellation of ideas related to the following:

- "customary law" such as is described in Bruce Benson's "The
Enterprise Law," in which multiple legal systems coexist and reach
decisions by their own processes. Also called "market law" or
"polycentric law." Polycentric meaning "many centers."

- the opposite of PPL/polycentric/market law is the kind of
centralized law we now have. Analogous to a "command economy."

- the "Law Merchant" of the high seas is an example, in which no
single nation could impose its national laws is an example. Some form
of legal system nevertheless existed, in a kind of emergent process
(which apparently lives on in the "Universal Commercial Code," the
UCC, which many nations adhere to.).

- criminal enterprises (Mafia, Triads, Tongs) of course must use
polycentric, local law, because the central law has declared them
illegal. That certain standards of justice and honor exist is evidence
that this can work (the role of reputation-based systems, of future
expected business, etc., is also crucial to how such ventures work).

- corporations, clubs, mailing lists like this one, are all examples
of where polycentril law is critical.

- a fictional treatment of PPLs can be found in the seminal novel
"Snow Crash," by Neal Stephenson, in which multiple legal systems
exist and compete with each other, sometimes fighting, mostly reaching
some degree of coexistence. For example, "Mr. Lee's Greater Hong Kong"
is one such "protection racket" that exists in the L.A. area.

- the mention of "protection rackets" above is not accidental: the
idea of PPLs is that one signs up, pays a fee, and is then
more-or-less protected by that agency. In "Snow Crash," as a matter of
fact, the Mafia is just another PPL. (I'm using PPL as a noun here, a
la the synechdoche process I mentioned several days ago.)

- the positive effects of PPLs derive from the meta-market in PPLs and
the fact that customers/subscribers must directly pay for the laws
they want enforced. Thus, if I joing a PPL that tracks down drug
abusers, that arrests them, jails them, and has to fight with _their_
PPL. Instead of passing zillions of laws and expecting _others_ to pay
for them (which is the current regulation-crazy environment we're in
today), market law forces people to "shop for" laws they actually want
enforced badly enough to pay for, and possibly to fight other PPLs
for.

Thus, "victimless crimes" are likely to be legalized, because few if
any people will actually shell out real money to enforce them. The
"basic laws" concerning murder, theft, rape, etc., the wisdom of the
ages, are likely to be the "Schelling points" of a PPL society.

Let me note that PPLs are the systems we use in our everyday life: in
the friends we have, the parties we go to (I always use the private
party as an example of a PPL--the host makes the rules and that's
that), the rules of our companies and institutions, and so on.

Thus, anarcho-capitalism and customary law go together nicely, and
probably inseparably.

A common misconception is that one can only be subject to the laws of
a PPL if one has joined that PPL. Thus, the only people who could be
affected by the decisions of Tim's Tribunal are those who joined
it. No, such things, in List parlance, would be the "societies" that
many people were proposing eight or ten months ago, as in "The Polite
Society."

In reality, the idea of PPLs is that they extend their laws as they
see fit, but then must deal with the consequences of their actions.
Thus, various PPLs must deal with each other, in various ways.

(Tim's Tribunal needs very little from other PPLs, such as Mike
Price's "Sheriff" system, the List not being a very realistic
simulation of the real world and all, and so my Tribunal can just go
ahead and do its thing.)

